WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
Sixty-four of us are going on the trip.

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.600
A minibus seats fourteen for three

3
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.620
hundred coins, a bus thirty for

4
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:09.760
five hundred and fifty.

5
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.060
The maths says two point one three buses.

6
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.080
What do I do with point

7
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.280
one three of a bus?

8
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.750
Today: whole numbers and yes-or-no

9
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.570
choices, and why rounding isn’t enough.

10
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.230
Some decisions come only in whole

11
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.570
numbers: buses, beds, teachers, desks.

12
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.730
Others are yes or no: take

13
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.150
this load, or leave it.

14
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.320
A linear program allows

15
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.320
fractions, which makes it fast.

16
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.690
But you can’t hire part of a bus.

17
00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:46.930
So round it?

18
00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:50.570
Round down to two buses, and four people

19
00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.350
stay behind: the plan breaks a limit.

20
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.990
Round up to three buses, and it costs one

21
00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.910
thousand six hundred and fifty coins.

22
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:00.760
Quick check!

23
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.500
The maths said two point one

24
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.980
three buses, and I booked two.

25
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:07.450
What went wrong?

26
00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.510
Four people had no seat.

27
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.200
Search the whole-number plans instead: two

28
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.840
buses and a minibus seat seventy-four, for

29
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.460
one thousand four hundred coins.

30
00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.590
That’s the cheapest plan

31
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.670
that seats everyone.

32
00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.320
A model whose variables must be whole

33
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.420
numbers is an integer program.

34
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.320
When they can only be zero or one,

35
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.200
they are yes-or-no variables.

36
00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.770
Integer programs are much harder, because

37
00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.870
the best plan is no longer at a corner.

38
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.800
Back to the truck for the village

39
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.920
school: six loads, one thousand kilos.

40
00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.890
Each load gets a yes-or-no variable: one

41
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.610
means take it, zero means leave it.

42
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.150
The weights taken must add up

43
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.970
to at most a thousand.

44
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.080
The goal: the most points.

45
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:02.430
In Python, with Google’s OR-Tools,

46
00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.530
that model is a few lines.

47
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.820
One yes-or-no variable per load, one

48
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.840
limit on the weight, one goal.

49
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.280
The solver answers: desks and books,

50
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.500
sixty-five points, a thousand kilos

51
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.100
exactly.

52
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.970
A quick rule, most points per kilo

53
00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:23.810
first, scores only sixty.

54
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.900
Integer solvers search more cleverly.

55
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.060
Dynamic programming fills a table of

56
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.620
smaller trucks, and branch and bound

57
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.420
skips plans that can’t win.

58
00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.390
But look at what stays behind: the solar

59
00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.790
panels, the water filter, the footballs.

60
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.340
The points are a judgement: who set them,

61
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.080
and did the village school have a say?

62
00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.290
If the filter keeps pupils

63
00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.450
healthy, maybe it must go.

64
00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.500
In the app, pack the truck

65
00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.920
greedily, then show the best load.

66
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.090
Open the table underneath and follow the

67
00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.270
outlined cells back from the corner: each

68
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.350
step takes a load or skips it.

69
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.180
Your turn.

70
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.060
The village school says the water

71
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.920
filter must go on the truck.

72
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.100
In the model, what changes?

73
00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:19.350
A new limit: take-filter equals one.

74
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.940
Remember: whole numbers and yes-or-no

75
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.220
choices need an integer program.

76
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.370
Rounding can break a limit or cost more.

77
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.210
And people decide what the points mean.

78
00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.730
Try it in class tomorrow: plan the

79
00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.130
trip buses with your pupils.

80
00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.610
Give them the people, the

81
00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.090
seats and the prices.

82
00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.860
Groups find the cheapest whole-number

83
00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.440
plan that seats everyone, then compare

84
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.680
it with rounding the fraction.
